2a2e314a [simon@midnightblue.local] currently i would like te feed 24MB of logs from the tool back into itself for summary of sessions
2a2e33f8 [simon@midnightblue.local] pehaps a two pass algorithm could be good so entire logs are procesed twice?
2a2e3486 [deepseek-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.002 5.066KB 8.61s
2a2e3486 [deepseek-flash] #Two passes is a sound instinct, but the win comes from what each pass operates on, not from
2a2e3486 [deepseek-flash] #re-reading the raw logs twice. Re-reading 24MB of raw text twice is usually the expensive,
2a2e3486 [deepseek-flash] #low-value version.
2a2e3486 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e3486 [deepseek-flash] #• Reality check on size*
2a2e3486 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e3486 [deepseek-flash] #24MB is roughly 5–7M tokens. That is several times any current context window, so chunking is
2a2e3487 [deepseek-flash] #unavoidable, and a full raw second pass roughly doubles input cost. Before designing around that,
2a2e3487 [deepseek-flash] #check whether you can shrink the input:
2a2e3487 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e3487 [deepseek-flash] #- Group by session ID first. If sessions are the unit of continuity, many will be short and need no
2a2e3487 [deepseek-flash] #chunking at all. Only long sessions hit the chunking path.
2a2e3487 [deepseek-flash] #- Deduplicate by log template. Logs are highly repetitive; template extraction often cuts volume by
2a2e3487 [deepseek-flash] #5–20x with no loss of signal.
2a2e3487 [deepseek-flash] #- Filter to severity/level or time window if the summary only needs notable events.
2a2e3487 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e3487 [deepseek-flash] #If you can get from 24MB to ~2–4MB of distinct content, the whole problem gets easier and a second
2a2e3487 [deepseek-flash] #pass becomes cheap.
2a2e3487 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e3487 [deepseek-flash] #• Recommended two-pass shape*
2a2e3487 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e3487 [deepseek-flash] #Pass 1 — extract, don’t summarize prose.
2a2e3487 [deepseek-flash] #- Walk chunks in order, one per session (or per session segment).
2a2e3487 [deepseek-flash] #- Emit structured records, not flowing text: timestamped events, errors with first/last seen,
2a2e3487 [deepseek-flash] #counts, session boundaries, entities, open questions, and a pointer (file + byte/line offset) to
2a2e3487 [deepseek-flash] #the raw range.
2a2e3487 [deepseek-flash] #- This pass is deterministic-ish and cheap to validate.
2a2e3487 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e3487 [deepseek-flash] #Pass 2 — synthesize per session.
2a2e3487 [deepseek-flash] #- Input is the structured records from pass 1, not the raw logs.
2a2e3487 [deepseek-flash] #- Produce the session summary, timeline, and open items.
2a2e3487 [deepseek-flash] #- If a session’s records look ambiguous, contradictory, or high-severity, then do a
2a2e3488 [deepseek-flash] #targeted raw re-read of just that session’s byte range. That gives you the benefit of a second raw
2a2e3488 [deepseek-flash] #pass without paying for it everywhere.
2a2e3488 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e3488 [deepseek-flash] #So: pass 1 over all raw (once), pass 2 over compressed artifacts, plus selective raw re-reads. That
2a2e3488 [deepseek-flash] #is usually better than two full raw passes.
2a2e3488 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e3488 [deepseek-flash] #• Where a full second raw pass is justified*
2a2e3488 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e3488 [deepseek-flash] #Only if you need continuity that the first pass cannot capture, for example:
2a2e3488 [deepseek-flash] #- Later events change the meaning of earlier ones (retry storms, cascading failures).
2a2e3488 [deepseek-flash] #- You need cross-session correlation that only emerges once everything is seen.
2a2e3488 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e3488 [deepseek-flash] #In that case, do pass 1 to build a skeleton/summary, then pass 2 re-reads raw chunks with the
2a2e3488 [deepseek-flash] #skeleton as context. That is genuinely a second raw pass, but the context you supply should be
2a2e3488 [deepseek-flash] #the compact skeleton, not the growing full history. Keep the injected context bounded.
2a2e3488 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e3488 [deepseek-flash] #• Continuity mechanics that matter either way*
2a2e3488 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e3488 [deepseek-flash] #- Bounded rolling summary, re-compressed every N chunks. Do not let the summary grow unbounded in
2a2e3488 [deepseek-flash] #context.
2a2e3488 [deepseek-flash] #- Overlap only at atomic-event boundaries (stack traces, multi-line JSON, sessions). Dedup
2a2e3488 [deepseek-flash] #overlapping events by hash or event ID.
2a2e3488 [deepseek-flash] #- Store chunk outputs keyed by session and offset so you can re-run a single session without
2a2e3488 [deepseek-flash] #reprocessing everything.
2a2e3488 [deepseek-flash] #- Keep an evaluation set of sessions whose summaries you can check, including ones that span chunk
2a2e3488 [deepseek-flash] #boundaries.
2a2e3489 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e3489 [deepseek-flash] #• Open questions specific to your case*
2a2e3489 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e3489 [deepseek-flash] #- What defines a “session” in these logs — a session ID field, a time gap, a request chain, or
2a2e3489 [deepseek-flash] #something inferred?
2a2e3489 [deepseek-flash] #- Do you need exact citations back to raw lines, or is a prose summary enough?
2a2e3489 [deepseek-flash] #- Is the second pass meant to improve accuracy, or just to roll up per-chunk summaries? Those are
2a2e3489 [deepseek-flash] #different designs.
2a2e3489 [deepseek-flash] #- Can you run passes in parallel per session, or does ordering matter for your continuity?
2a2e3489 [deepseek-flash] #- What is your token/cost ceiling per run, and how often will this run?
2a2e3489 [deepseek-flash] #- Do you have a known-good session summary to evaluate against?
2a2e3489 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e3489 [deepseek-flash] #If you tell me the log format (does it have session IDs? is it line-JSON? multiline stack traces?)
2a2e3489 [deepseek-flash] #and the summary output you want, I can make the pass structure and chunk boundaries concrete.
2a2e3489 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e376f [simon@midnightblue.local] i will attach source of tool and then some examples logs
2a2e3905 [deepseek-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.007 168.7KB 20.87s
2a2e3905 [deepseek-flash] #Reading the source changes the answer, because your log format already gives you most of what
2a2e3905 [deepseek-flash] #chunking algorithms usually have to invent.
2a2e3905 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e3905 [deepseek-flash] #• What your format already provides*
2a2e3905 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e3905 [deepseek-flash] #Every line is `<8 hex slopmark> [source] content`. That gives you:
2a2e3905 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e3905 [deepseek-flash] #- A parseable timestamp on every line (`parseInt(line.substring(0,8),16)`, sixteenths since
2a2e3905 [deepseek-flash] #2025-04-12).
2a2e3905 [deepseek-flash] #- A source tag in brackets — `[roha]`, `[ASSISTANT]`, `[RELAY]`, `[GEMINI]`, `[CLAUDE]`, `[FORGE]`,
2a2e3906 [deepseek-flash] #`[KOHA]`, `[FAIL]`, `[CRASH]`, `[SPEAK]`, `[TOOL]` — which separates user turns, model replies,
2a2e3906 [deepseek-flash] #tool calls, tool results, and harness chatter.
2a2e3906 [deepseek-flash] #- A session delimiter already in use: the bird-rune line `[roha] 𓅷 𓅸 𓅹 𓅺 𓅻 𓅼 𓅽`. Since
2a2e3906 [deepseek-flash] #`logForge` appends, that line marks each process run in `forge.log`.
2a2e3906 [deepseek-flash] #- A stronger unit you may not be using: `forge/transmission-.json`, written by `saveHistory`,
2a2e3906 [deepseek-flash] #is `rohaHistory` verbatim — a structured per-session record with roles, names, tool_calls, prices,
2a2e3906 [deepseek-flash] #elapsed. If your goal is session* summary, that is a far better input than re-parsing the raw
2a2e3906 [deepseek-flash] #log.
2a2e3906 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e3906 [deepseek-flash] #So the first design question is really: are you summarizing the conversation (use the
2a2e3906 [deepseek-flash] #transmission JSON), or the harness behaviour (use the raw log — crashes, retries, model
2a2e3906 [deepseek-flash] #resets, credit accounting)?
2a2e3906 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e3906 [deepseek-flash] #• Chunking algorithm for your format*
2a2e3906 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e3906 [deepseek-flash] #Do not chunk by bytes or tokens. Segment by the structure that is already there:
2a2e3906 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e3906 [deepseek-flash] #1. Session = lines between bird-rune markers. This is your top-level chunk. Sessions never
2a2e3906 [deepseek-flash] #need to share context across the boundary, so no raw overlap is required at this level.
2a2e3906 [deepseek-flash] #2. Turn = a `[roha]` line that is a user message, through to the next such line. A turn
2a2e3906 [deepseek-flash] #contains the user prompt, one or more `[ASSISTANT]` / model replies, `[RELAY]` tool calls, `[TOOL]`
2a2e3906 [deepseek-flash] #results, and any `[FAIL]` / `[CRASH]` that occurred. Turns are atomic — never split one.
2a2e3906 [deepseek-flash] #3. Sub-turn split only if a single turn exceeds your token budget. Split at `[RELAY]`
2a2e3906 [deepseek-flash] #tool-call boundaries or at blank-line paragraph breaks inside an assistant message. Never split a
2a2e3907 [deepseek-flash] #stack trace, a fenced code block, or a multi-line JSON blob.
2a2e3907 [deepseek-flash] #4. Noise filter first. Drop `[DEBUG]`, `[TABLE]`, `[UNICODE]`, `[GETDATE]`, `[TEST2-4]`,
2a2e3907 [deepseek-flash] #`[MULTI]`, `[STRIP]`, `[LOG]` chatter unless you specifically need it. On a typical 24MB forge.log
2a2e3907 [deepseek-flash] #this alone removes a large fraction of bytes without losing signal.
2a2e3907 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e3907 [deepseek-flash] #• Two-pass, tuned to this*
2a2e3907 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e3907 [deepseek-flash] #Pass 1 — deterministic, no model call. Walk the log once, emit one record per session:
2a2e3907 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e3907 [deepseek-flash] #- `sessionId` (hash of first slopmark + byte offset)
2a2e3907 [deepseek-flash] #- `startSlopmark`, `endSlopmark`, `byteRange` in the source file
2a2e3907 [deepseek-flash] #- `sourceCounts` — a `{}` like your existing `days[date].tags`
2a2e3907 [deepseek-flash] #- `turns[]` — each with `userSlopmark`, `userText`, `assistantReplies[]`, `toolCalls[]`,
2a2e3907 [deepseek-flash] #`toolResults[]`, `errors[]`
2a2e3907 [deepseek-flash] #- `modelSwitches[]` — every `[RELAY] model reset` line
2a2e3907 [deepseek-flash] #- `failures[]` — `[FAIL]`, `[CRASH]`, `unhandled error`, `maximum prompt length`, the specific
2a2e3907 [deepseek-flash] #error strings your `relay()` catch block matches on
2a2e3907 [deepseek-flash] #- `shares[]` — paths from `[KOHA] Share file path:` lines
2a2e3907 [deepseek-flash] #- `pricing` — sum of any `$` amounts you log
2a2e3907 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e3907 [deepseek-flash] #This pass replaces and cleans up the logic already sitting in `readHistory` and `stripLog`. Those
2a2e3907 [deepseek-flash] #two duplicate parsing, and `readHistory` has a few sharp edges worth fixing while you are in there:
2a2e3907 [deepseek-flash] #the `if(!min || secs<min)` guard breaks on a legitimate `secs==0`, and the session/tags push
2a2e3907 [deepseek-flash] #ordering only works because the bird line happens to start the next session rather than end the
2a2e3907 [deepseek-flash] #previous one.
2a2e3907 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e3908 [deepseek-flash] #Pass 2 — per session, one model call. Feed the session record, or the raw session lines if they fit
2a2e3908 [deepseek-flash] #comfortably. Ask for a bounded summary against a fixed schema (timeline, decisions, unresolved
2a2e3908 [deepseek-flash] #items, failures, entities, open questions). Store it keyed by `sessionId`.
2a2e3908 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e3908 [deepseek-flash] #Pass 3 — optional roll-up. Feed the session summaries (not raw logs) plus a compact global
2a2e3908 [deepseek-flash] #timeline into one call. This is where you get cross-session continuity cheaply.
2a2e3908 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e3908 [deepseek-flash] #• Overlap*
2a2e3908 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e3908 [deepseek-flash] #Not needed at session boundaries, and not needed at turn boundaries, because both are
2a2e3908 [deepseek-flash] #self-contained. Raw overlap is only justified when you must split mid-turn on a token budget, and
2a2e3908 [deepseek-flash] #even then, overlap by the last complete tool-call/tool-result pair, not by a percentage of lines.
2a2e3908 [deepseek-flash] #Deduplicate by the 8-hex slopmark plus source tag, which is unique enough for a line.
2a2e3908 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e3908 [deepseek-flash] #• Growing summary*
2a2e3908 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e3908 [deepseek-flash] #No. Keep the rolling summary bounded and outside the model context. For each session summarization
2a2e3908 [deepseek-flash] #call, inject a fixed-size context: the previous two or three session summaries plus the current
2a2e3908 [deepseek-flash] #session record. Re-compress every N sessions. Store all per-session summaries in `forge/` next to
2a2e3908 [deepseek-flash] #`transmission-*.json` so a single session can be re-run without touching the other 23MB.
2a2e3908 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e3908 [deepseek-flash] #• Questions worth answering before you build*
2a2e3908 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e3908 [deepseek-flash] #- Is the summary target the conversation (`transmission-*.json`) or the harness log (crashes, model
2a2e3908 [deepseek-flash] #resets, retries)? The two produce very different pass-1 records.
2a2e3909 [deepseek-flash] #- Do you need to distinguish the user's `[roha]` lines from the harness's `[roha]` status lines?
2a2e3909 [deepseek-flash] #Right now both share the tag; the id passed to `logForge` (`rohaUser` vs `"roha"`) is the only
2a2e3909 [deepseek-flash] #discriminator, and it is not written into the line.
2a2e3909 [deepseek-flash] #- What is the token budget per session call, and what is the model you plan to run pass 2 on? The
2a2e3909 [deepseek-flash] #`defaultModel` and the cheap models in `modelSpecs` will produce very different quality on dense
2a2e3909 [deepseek-flash] #log text.
2a2e3909 [deepseek-flash] #- Do you need citations back to line offsets, or is a prose summary enough? If citations matter,
2a2e3909 [deepseek-flash] #the `byteRange` in the pass-1 record is what makes it cheap.
2a2e3909 [deepseek-flash] #- How do you evaluate continuity? A held-out set of sessions whose summaries you have already
2a2e3909 [deepseek-flash] #reviewed by hand is the only way to catch drift.
2a2e3909 [deepseek-flash] #- Do you want pass 2 to be re-runnable per session without reprocessing? If yes, pass 1 must write
2a2e3909 [deepseek-flash] #one file per session, not one big JSON.
2a2e3909 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e3909 [deepseek-flash] #If you tell me which of those two summary targets you actually want, the pass-1 record and the
2a2e3909 [deepseek-flash] #segmentation rules become concrete enough to sketch as functions.
2a2e3909 [deepseek-flash] #
