2a2e413e [deepseek-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.011 181.7KB 33.41s
2a2e413e [deepseek-flash] #Two things to get out of the way first: the top two lines are paste artefacts, not log lines.
2a2e413e [deepseek-flash] #`source env3/bin/activateyolo task=obb ...` and `oops` have no 8-hex prefix and no `[source]` tag,
2a2e413e [deepseek-flash] #so they are your shell prompt/history bleeding into the copy. Your parser should reject any line
2a2e413e [deepseek-flash] #that does not match `^[0-9a-f]{8} \[[^\]]+\] `. That single rule also drops the wrapped fragments
2a2e413e [deepseek-flash] #you can see mid-paste like `a full raw seco\nnd pass` — actually no, that one is a real log line,
2a2e413e [deepseek-flash] #it just lost its prefix in the paste. Which brings up the first correction.
2a2e413e [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e413e [deepseek-flash] #• Correction 1: the slopmark is not a line identifier*
2a2e413e [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e413e [deepseek-flash] #I suggested earlier that you could dedupe overlapping lines by slopmark plus source tag. That was
2a2e413e [deepseek-flash] #wrong. `logForge` computes `time` once per call and applies it to every split line, and `flush`
2a2e413e [deepseek-flash] #calls `logForge` once per `printBuffer` entry. So a single assistant message is dozens of log lines
2a2e413e [deepseek-flash] #that share one slopmark, and the slopmark drifts mid-message when the flush crosses a 62.5 ms tick
2a2e413e [deepseek-flash] #— you can see that in your sample: `2a2e3486` for the status and first content line, then
2a2e413e [deepseek-flash] #`2a2e3487`, `2a2e3488`, `2a2e3489` as the reply wraps. The slopmark is a flush timestamp, not a
2a2e413e [deepseek-flash] #record key. If you need a per-line key, use `(slopmark, source, ordinal within source run)`.
2a2e413e [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e413e [deepseek-flash] #• Correction 2: the log is not a faithful copy of the conversation*
2a2e413f [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e413f [deepseek-flash] #`mdToAnsi` consumes code-fence markers and emits ANSI background codes for code blocks; `logForge`
2a2e413f [deepseek-flash] #then strips ANSI. So in `forge.log` a fenced code block becomes unmarked indented-looking text with
2a2e413f [deepseek-flash] #no delimiters, and word wrap has already broken lines at 100 columns. That means reconstructing the
2a2e413f [deepseek-flash] #assistant message from the log is lossy for anything code-heavy. `forge/transmission-*.json` does
2a2e413f [deepseek-flash] #not have this problem. If your summary target is the conversation, parse the transmission JSON. Use
2a2e413f [deepseek-flash] #`forge.log` only for harness behaviour: shares, model switches, failures, cost, credit.
2a2e413f [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e413f [deepseek-flash] #• Correction 3: the status line is a free structured record*
2a2e413f [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e413f [deepseek-flash] #`2a2e3486 [deepseek-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.002 5.066KB
2a2e413f [deepseek-flash] #8.61s`
2a2e413f [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e413f [deepseek-flash] #That is `rohaTitle`, `rohaModel`, provider emoji, forge glyph, temperature, cost, history size,
2a2e413f [deepseek-flash] #elapsed. The `🪠` means tools were off for that turn (`🐸` would mean tools on but functions
2a2e413f [deepseek-flash] #failed, the pail would mean active). Pass 1 should parse every line matching `^#🟠.*nitrologic
2a2e413f [deepseek-flash] #Relay` into a numeric turn record. You get per-turn spend, latency, and context size with no model
2a2e413f [deepseek-flash] #call at all.
2a2e413f [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e413f [deepseek-flash] #• What the sample already tells you about your own problem*
2a2e413f [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e413f [deepseek-flash] #The two status lines read `5.066KB` and `168.7KB`. That jump is the `relay.ts` share (137044 bytes)
2a2e413f [deepseek-flash] #plus the previous reply. The history is growing exactly the way you were worried about in your
2a2e413f [deepseek-flash] #earlier question, and it is visible in the log. The `maximum prompt length exceeded` path in your
2a2e413f [deepseek-flash] #catch block is the eventual outcome. A bounded per-session summary stored outside `rohaHistory` is
2a2e4140 [deepseek-flash] #the fix, and the status line gives you the metric to watch it.
2a2e4140 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e4140 [deepseek-flash] #• Pass-1 rules that fall out of the sample*
2a2e4140 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e4140 [deepseek-flash] #Segment by source-run, not by line count. A turn is:
2a2e4140 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e4140 [deepseek-flash] #- one or more consecutive `[simon@midnightblue.local]` lines — these are the user's input lines,
2a2e4140 [deepseek-flash] #joined with `\r\n` before relay, but logged individually with their own slopmarks;
2a2e4140 [deepseek-flash] #- then a run of `[deepseek-flash]` lines whose first member matches the status pattern, followed by
2a2e4140 [deepseek-flash] #content lines to be joined in order after stripping the leading `#`;
2a2e4140 [deepseek-flash] #- any `[roha]`, `[FAIL]`, `[CRASH]`, `[TOOL]`, `[RELAY]` lines interleaved belong to the same turn;
2a2e4140 [deepseek-flash] #- the turn ends at the next user line or the bird-rune session marker.
2a2e4140 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e4140 [deepseek-flash] #Session boundary is still `[roha] 𓅷 𓅸 𓅹 𓅺 𓅻 𓅼 𓅽`, which your `readHistory` already keys on.
2a2e4140 [deepseek-flash] #Nothing in the sample crosses one, so this is a single open session.
2a2e4140 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e4140 [deepseek-flash] #• One question this raises*
2a2e4140 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e4140 [deepseek-flash] #Do you want pass 1 to read `forge.log`, the transmission JSON, or both joined on slopmark range?
2a2e4140 [deepseek-flash] #The transmission JSON has clean message boundaries and preserves code fences; the log has harness
2a2e4140 [deepseek-flash] #events the JSON does not (shares, model resets, credit deltas, the status metrics). If you want
2a2e4140 [deepseek-flash] #both in one record, the join key is the session's slopmark range, which the bird markers give you.
2a2e4140 [deepseek-flash] #
