2a2e51d3 [deepseek-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.015 191.8KB 44.68s
2a2e51d3 [deepseek-flash] #refine.ts is already the pass-1 shape I described — deterministic, single pass, no model calls — so
2a2e51d3 [deepseek-flash] #the useful thing is to say where it diverges from what a pass-2 summarizer needs, and which parts
2a2e51d3 [deepseek-flash] #are quietly wrong.
2a2e51d3 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e51d3 [deepseek-flash] #• Bugs that will bite*
2a2e51d3 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e51d3 [deepseek-flash] #1. `reduce` is declared `async` and returns `Promise<number>`, but nothing inside awaits anything,
2a2e51d3 [deepseek-flash] #and the caller does not await it. So `count` in the per-file `console.log` prints a Promise. The
2a2e51d3 [deepseek-flash] #`Deno.writeTextFile` inside is also unawaited, so writes race with the loop and with the final
2a2e51d3 [deepseek-flash] #summary print. Either make the function synchronous or await both.
2a2e51d3 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e51d3 [deepseek-flash] #2. `history/` is never created. There is no `mkdir` anywhere in the file. If the directory is
2a2e51d3 [deepseek-flash] #missing, every write throws into the catch, you log `[REFINE] error`, and you produce nothing while
2a2e51d4 [deepseek-flash] #appearing to succeed.
2a2e51d4 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e51d4 [deepseek-flash] #3. Session detection keys on the literal substring `[roha] [FORGE]`, which only appears when
2a2e51d4 [deepseek-flash] #`echoInfo` is on (verbose or debugging) or when the model list changes. In the log sample you
2a2e51d4 [deepseek-flash] #shared there is no such line at all, so `sessionCount` would be 0 for that run. That cascades:
2a2e51d4 [deepseek-flash] #`stats.recent!=sessionCount` is `0!=0`, so the per-model `sessions` counter never increments and
2a2e51d4 [deepseek-flash] #the model histogram reports `sessions:0` for every model. The reliable marker is already in the log
2a2e51d4 [deepseek-flash] #— the bird-rune line emitted once by `echo(birds)` at startup, which is also what `readHistory`
2a2e51d4 [deepseek-flash] #keys on.
2a2e51d4 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e51d4 [deepseek-flash] #4. The failure branch in `parseHeader` is dead. `if(words[1]=="failure")` expects the second word
2a2e51d4 [deepseek-flash] #to be "failure", but the only failure line emitted is `[FORGE] enumerateModels endpoint failure for
2a2e51d4 [deepseek-flash] #account X`, so `words[0]` is "enumerateModels" and `words[1]` is "endpoint". No vendor failures are
2a2e51d4 [deepseek-flash] #ever recorded, which makes the later `maxTime==0` cleanup moot.
2a2e51d4 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e51d4 [deepseek-flash] #5. The `Connected to` parse is brittle. The line is `Connected to deepseek (13) 0.48s`, so
2a2e51d4 [deepseek-flash] #`parseInt("(13)")` is NaN. It happens to be unused, but the parsing is one edit away from a silent
2a2e51d4 [deepseek-flash] #error.
2a2e51d4 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e51d4 [deepseek-flash] #6. `minDate`/`maxDate` only update for kept lines, because `slopDate` is called after the filters.
2a2e51d4 [deepseek-flash] #Blanks, `[roha]`, `[remote]`, `[PORT]` and `[FAIL]` lines do not contribute. The reported range is
2a2e51d4 [deepseek-flash] #therefore narrower than the log, and if a run ends on a crash the end time is wrong.
2a2e51d4 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e51d4 [deepseek-flash] #7. `bigCount` is incremented and never reported; the `continue` silently drops the line.
2a2e51d5 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e51d5 [deepseek-flash] #• What it does not yet do, for feeding a model*
2a2e51d5 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e51d5 [deepseek-flash] #It flattens but does not rejoin. A single assistant reply is dozens of log lines, each with its own
2a2e51d5 [deepseek-flash] #slopmark and its own output record, so your history file presents each 100-column fragment as a
2a2e51d5 [deepseek-flash] #separate event. Pass 2 will read a paragraph as a dozen unrelated observations. The join rule is:
2a2e51d5 [deepseek-flash] #within one source tag, a status line starts a new message and everything from the same source until
2a2e51d5 [deepseek-flash] #the next status line or source change belongs to it. Consecutive `[simon@...]` lines are one
2a2e51d5 [deepseek-flash] #prompt.
2a2e51d5 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e51d5 [deepseek-flash] #It drops the slopmark. The output keeps a formatted date but not the raw sixteenths, which loses
2a2e51d5 [deepseek-flash] #sub-second ordering and the only cheap rejoin key you have. The `#` prefix that `logForge` adds to
2a2e51d5 [deepseek-flash] #model output is a cleaner discriminator than the current `@`/`:` heuristic, and it survives into
2a2e51d5 [deepseek-flash] #`trim2`.
2a2e51d5 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e51d5 [deepseek-flash] #It ignores the status line. `#🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.002 5.066KB
2a2e51d5 [deepseek-flash] #8.61s` is a complete per-turn record — model, provider, tools state, temperature, cost, context
2a2e51d5 [deepseek-flash] #size, latency. Parsing it gives you a numeric timeline with no model call, and it is exactly the
2a2e51d5 [deepseek-flash] #metric that shows the context growing from 5KB to 169KB in your sample before the
2a2e51d5 [deepseek-flash] #maximum-prompt-length path fires.
2a2e51d5 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e51d5 [deepseek-flash] #It emits one flat file per input file. For pass 2 you want one record per session, keyed by a
2a2e51d5 [deepseek-flash] #stable id derived from the start slopmark and the source file, so a single bad summary can be
2a2e51d5 [deepseek-flash] #regenerated without re-reading the whole corpus.
2a2e51d5 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e51d5 [deepseek-flash] #• One scoping question*
2a2e51d6 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e51d6 [deepseek-flash] #refine.ts lives in `nitrologic.github.io` and writes to `history/`, which looks like a publication
2a2e51d6 [deepseek-flash] #pipeline rather than a summarization pipeline. If that is right, leave it alone and add a separate
2a2e51d6 [deepseek-flash] #pass-1 that emits structured per-session records for the model. Publication wants readable prose;
2a2e51d6 [deepseek-flash] #pass 2 wants records, offsets, and rejoin keys. Trying to serve both from one output is what will
2a2e51d6 [deepseek-flash] #make the summarizer's input awkward.
2a2e51d6 [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e5b2d [roha] [KOHA] Share file path: /Users/simon/nitrologic/nitrologic.github.io/distill.ts  size: 4421
2a2e5cbc [simon@midnightblue.local] agree, here is one more tool i have been using. will form plan based on your feedback, many thanks
2a2e5d6a [deepseek-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.004 201.8KB 8.25s
2a2e5d6a [deepseek-flash] #distill.ts fixes one thing from refine.ts and introduces a different one.
2a2e5d6a [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e5d6a [deepseek-flash] #• Fixed:* `distill` is now awaited at the call site. The unawaited-write race is gone,
2a2e5d6a [deepseek-flash] #though only because the write is commented out.
2a2e5d6a [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e5d6a [deepseek-flash] #• New bug, and it is the important one:* the loop enumerates `history/` but reads `raw/` +
2a2e5d6a [deepseek-flash] #`file.name`. So the refined output is never read. The pipeline is raw → refine → history → distill,
2a2e5d6a [deepseek-flash] #but distill is re-reading raw and ignoring what refine produced. Either that is intentional
2a2e5d6a [deepseek-flash] #(distill is meant to be an independent pass over raw with a different filter) or the read path
2a2e5d6a [deepseek-flash] #should be `history/`. Given the file comment says "for all files in history/", it looks like a
2a2e5d6a [deepseek-flash] #leftover.
2a2e5d6a [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e5d6b [deepseek-flash] #• Second bug, consequences of the first:* with the write commented out, distill produces no
2a2e5d6b [deepseek-flash] #output at all. It is now a metrics-only pass — vendor connections, model session counts, fail
2a2e5d6b [deepseek-flash] #count, line count — printed to console. If the intent was "refine strips noise, distill keeps only
2a2e5d6b [deepseek-flash] #recent signal", that second stage no longer exists. If the intent was "distill just reports", then
2a2e5d6b [deepseek-flash] #the name is misleading and the `result` array, `slopDate`, and `line2` construction are all dead
2a2e5d6b [deepseek-flash] #weight.
2a2e5d6b [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e5d6b [deepseek-flash] #• Carried over unchanged from refine.ts:*
2a2e5d6b [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e5d6b [deepseek-flash] #- Session detection still keys on the literal `[roha] [FORGE]`, which only appears under
2a2e5d6b [deepseek-flash] #verbose/debugging. Same cascade: `sessionCount` stays 0, so `stats.sessions` never increments and
2a2e5d6b [deepseek-flash] #the model histogram is meaningless.
2a2e5d6b [deepseek-flash] #- The vendor failure branch is still dead (`words[1]=="failure"` never matches `enumerateModels
2a2e5d6b [deepseek-flash] #endpoint failure for account X`).
2a2e5d6b [deepseek-flash] #- `Connected to` still parses `(13)` as NaN.
2a2e5d6b [deepseek-flash] #- `bigCount` is still counted and never reported.
2a2e5d6b [deepseek-flash] #- No `mkdir`, though now the risk is on the read side: if `history/` does not exist,
2a2e5d6b [deepseek-flash] #`Deno.readDir("history")` throws before the loop and nothing runs.
2a2e5d6b [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e5d6b [deepseek-flash] #• One behaviour worth noting: `minDate`/`maxDate` now only advance for lines that
2a2e5d6b [deepseek-flash] #survive the 72-hour filter and the tag filter. So the printed range is the range of retained*
2a2e5d6b [deepseek-flash] #lines, not the range of the input. With a 72-hour window, min will always sit near the cutoff. That
2a2e5d6c [deepseek-flash] #is fine if you know it, misleading if you read it as the log's coverage.
2a2e5d6c [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e5d6c [deepseek-flash] #• For the plan*
2a2e5d6c [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e5d6c [deepseek-flash] #The shape that falls out of all three tools is:
2a2e5d6c [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e5d6c [deepseek-flash] #- pass 0, structural: parse `<8hex> [source] content`, reject lines that do not match, join wrapped
2a2e5d6c [deepseek-flash] #fragments within a source run, parse the `#🟠` status line into a per-turn numeric record, keep the
2a2e5d6c [deepseek-flash] #raw slopmark as the rejoin key;
2a2e5d6c [deepseek-flash] #- pass 1, segmentation: split on the bird-rune session marker and on source-tag changes, emit one
2a2e5d6c [deepseek-flash] #record per session with byte offsets and per-turn metrics — no model call;
2a2e5d6c [deepseek-flash] #- pass 2, per-session summary: one bounded model call per session record, output keyed by session
2a2e5d6c [deepseek-flash] #id so a single session can be regenerated;
2a2e5d6c [deepseek-flash] #- pass 3, roll-up: feed the session summaries, not raw logs, into one call for cross-session
2a2e5d6c [deepseek-flash] #continuity.
2a2e5d6c [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e5d6c [deepseek-flash] #refine.ts and distill.ts are both trying to be pass 0 and pass 1 at once, which is why they end up
2a2e5d6c [deepseek-flash] #dropping the slopmark, mis-detecting sessions, and producing output that is readable by a human but
2a2e5d6c [deepseek-flash] #awkward for a model. Splitting them removes most of the bugs above rather than patching them.
2a2e5d6c [deepseek-flash] #
2a2e5d6c [deepseek-flash] #Good luck with it.
2a2e5d6c [deepseek-flash] #
