2b93c6e9 [claude-haiku-5-5] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 claude-haiku-5-5 A\ 🪠 🧊 $0.002 2.609KB 5.42s
2b93c6e9 [claude-haiku-5-5] #You're right, my earlier answer only covered manual extended thinking with `budget_tokens`. Your
2b93c6e9 [claude-haiku-5-5] #snippet uses adaptive thinking, which works differently:
2b93c6e9 [claude-haiku-5-5] #
2b93c6e9 [claude-haiku-5-5] #- `thinking: {type: "adaptive"}` lets the model decide whether and how much to think for
2b93c6e9 [claude-haiku-5-5] #each request, so you don't set a token budget. `budget_tokens` doesn't apply in this mode.
2b93c6e9 [claude-haiku-5-5] #- `output_config: {effort: "high"}` guides how thoroughly Claude reasons and responds.
2b93c6e9 [claude-haiku-5-5] #Higher effort generally means more thinking and more output tokens, so it affects cost and latency.
2b93c6e9 [claude-haiku-5-5] #
2b93c6ea [claude-haiku-5-5] #For logging, the same principle holds: capture any `thinking` blocks from the response along with
2b93c6ea [claude-haiku-5-5] #the request ID and final text. With adaptive thinking, the amount of reasoning varies per request,
2b93c6ea [claude-haiku-5-5] #so your logs may show thinking on some calls and little or none on others. Also check whether
2b93c6ea [claude-haiku-5-5] #thinking content is returned in full or summarized for the model you're using, since that
2b93c6ea [claude-haiku-5-5] #determines what you can actually log.
2b93c6ea [claude-haiku-5-5] #
2b93c6ea [claude-haiku-5-5] #Support for `adaptive` and the `effort` option depends on the model, and these parameters have
2b93c6ea [claude-haiku-5-5] #changed across releases. I'd confirm the exact field names and supported values against Anthropic's
2b93c6ea [claude-haiku-5-5] #current extended thinking and effort documentation before relying on them.
2b93c6ea [claude-haiku-5-5] #
