2b93d2c0 [claude-haiku-5-5] #Thanks for the update. Testing thinking compatibility is a good way to confirm what your setup
2b93d2c0 [claude-haiku-5-5] #actually returns. A few things worth checking as you go:
2b93d2c0 [claude-haiku-5-5] #
2b93d2c0 [claude-haiku-5-5] #- Confirm the thinking config reached the API. Log the exact request body you send, not
2b93d2c0 [claude-haiku-5-5] #just your local object. A snippet like `request.thinking={...}` only works if your SDK or wrapper
2b93d2c0 [claude-haiku-5-5] #serializes it into the request; some wrappers drop unknown fields silently.
2b93d2c0 [claude-haiku-5-5] #- Check the response content types. Look for `thinking`, `redacted_thinking`, and `text`
2b93d2c0 [claude-haiku-5-5] #blocks, and record which appear. With adaptive thinking, some requests may return no thinking block
2b93d2c1 [claude-haiku-5-5] #at all, so an empty result on a simple prompt isn't necessarily a failure.
2b93d2c1 [claude-haiku-5-5] #- Compare effort levels. Run the same prompt with `effort` set to `low`, `medium`, and
2b93d2c1 [claude-haiku-5-5] #`high`, and compare the thinking content and `usage` token counts. This shows whether the setting
2b93d2c1 [claude-haiku-5-5] #is taking effect.
2b93d2c1 [claude-haiku-5-5] #- Watch for API errors. If the model or SDK version doesn't support `adaptive` or
2b93d2c1 [claude-haiku-5-5] #`output_config`, you'll usually get a 400-style validation error. That's a useful compatibility
2b93d2c1 [claude-haiku-5-5] #signal in itself.
2b93d2c1 [claude-haiku-5-5] #- Record the model ID and SDK version with each test, since support changes between
2b93d2c1 [claude-haiku-5-5] #releases.
2b93d2c1 [claude-haiku-5-5] #
2b93d2c1 [claude-haiku-5-5] #If you share the error message or the response's content block types, I can help you interpret
2b93d2c1 [claude-haiku-5-5] #them.
2b93d2c1 [claude-haiku-5-5] #
