2b93d2c1 [roha] [ASSISTANT] {"role":"assistant","mut":"claude-haiku-5-5","emoji":"A\\","name":"claude-haiku-5-5","content":"You can get Claude's reasoning by turning on **extended thinking** in your Messages API request. Set the `thinking` parameter to enabled and give it a `budget_tokens` value, which caps how many tokens Claude can spend thinking. The budget must be at least 1,024 and less than `max_tokens`.\n\nThe response then includes content blocks of type `thinking`, which contain the reasoning text, alongside the usual `text` blocks with the final answer. To log the chain of thought, capture the `thinking` blocks from the response and store them with the request ID and final answer. If you stream the response, the reasoning arrives as `thinking_delta` events that you can accumulate.\n\nA few things to keep in mind:\n\n- **Redacted blocks:** Some thinking may come back as `redacted_thinking` blocks, which contain encrypted content you can't read. Your logging should handle them without failing.\n- **Summaries:** On newer Claude 4 models, the returned thinking may be a summary of the full reasoning, while you are still billed for the full thinking tokens. Check the current docs for your model.\n- **Multi-turn and tool use:** If you send the conversation back, include the thinking blocks unchanged, especially when tool use is involved.\n- **Sensitive data:** Reasoning can echo user input or sensitive content, so apply the same retention and access controls you use for other logs.\n\nParameter names and supported options change between model versions, so check Anthropic's extended thinking documentation for the model you're using.","elapsed":6.553270958000009,"price":0.002611}
2b93d2c1 [roha] [ASSISTANT] {"role":"assistant","mut":"claude-haiku-5-5","emoji":"A\\","name":"claude-haiku-5-5","content":"You're right, my earlier answer only covered manual extended thinking with `budget_tokens`. Your snippet uses adaptive thinking, which works differently:\n\n- **`thinking: {type: \"adaptive\"}`** lets the model decide whether and how much to think for each request, so you don't set a token budget. `budget_tokens` doesn't apply in this mode.\n- **`output_config: {effort: \"high\"}`** guides how thoroughly Claude reasons and responds. Higher effort generally means more thinking and more output tokens, so it affects cost and latency.\n\nFor logging, the same principle holds: capture any `thinking` blocks from the response along with the request ID and final text. With adaptive thinking, the amount of reasoning varies per request, so your logs may show thinking on some calls and little or none on others. Also check whether thinking content is returned in full or summarized for the model you're using, since that determines what you can actually log.\n\nSupport for `adaptive` and the `effort` option depends on the model, and these parameters have changed across releases. I'd confirm the exact field names and supported values against Anthropic's current extended thinking and effort documentation before relying on them.","elapsed":5.416079333000002,"price":0.0024435}
2b93d2c1 [roha] [Anthropic] {"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","max_tokens":8192,"system":[{"type":"text","text":"As a guest assistant language model please be mindful of others, courteous and professional. Only post code on request and do not patronise. Use tabs when indenting source files. Prefer named reusable functions over inlining code with arrow and map style suggestions."},{"type":"text","text":"models under test include claude-haiku-5-5"}],"messages":[{"role":"user","content":"friend: hi claude"},{"role":"assistant","content":"Hi there! How can I help you today?"},{"role":"user","content":"friend: how do i enable chain of thought logging in claude api?"},{"role":"assistant","content":"You can get Claude's reasoning by turning on **extended thinking** in your Messages API request. Set the `thinking` parameter to enabled and give it a `budget_tokens` value, which caps how many tokens Claude can spend thinking. The budget must be at least 1,024 and less than `max_tokens`.\n\nThe response then includes content blocks of type `thinking`, which contain the reasoning text, alongside the usual `text` blocks with the final answer. To log the chain of thought, capture the `thinking` blocks from the response and store them with the request ID and final answer. If you stream the response, the reasoning arrives as `thinking_delta` events that you can accumulate.\n\nA few things to keep in mind:\n\n- **Redacted blocks:** Some thinking may come back as `redacted_thinking` blocks, which contain encrypted content you can't read. Your logging should handle them without failing.\n- **Summaries:** On newer Claude 4 models, the returned thinking may be a summary of the full reasoning, while you are still billed for the full thinking tokens. Check the current docs for your model.\n- **Multi-turn and tool use:** If you send the conversation back, include the thinking blocks unchanged, especially when tool use is involved.\n- **Sensitive data:** Reasoning can echo user input or sensitive content, so apply the same retention and access controls you use for other logs.\n\nParameter names and supported options change between model versions, so check Anthropic's extended thinking documentation for the model you're using."},{"role":"user","content":"friend: this is not enough?\r\nrequest.thinking={type:\"adaptive\"};\t\t\t\t\t\t\trequest.output_config={effort:\"high\"};"},{"role":"assistant","content":"You're right, my earlier answer only covered manual extended thinking with `budget_tokens`. Your snippet uses adaptive thinking, which works differently:\n\n- **`thinking: {type: \"adaptive\"}`** lets the model decide whether and how much to think for each request, so you don't set a token budget. `budget_tokens` doesn't apply in this mode.\n- **`output_config: {effort: \"high\"}`** guides how thoroughly Claude reasons and responds. Higher effort generally means more thinking and more output tokens, so it affects cost and latency.\n\nFor logging, the same principle holds: capture any `thinking` blocks from the response along with the request ID and final text. With adaptive thinking, the amount of reasoning varies per request, so your logs may show thinking on some calls and little or none on others. Also check whether thinking content is returned in full or summarized for the model you're using, since that determines what you can actually log.\n\nSupport for `adaptive` and the `effort` option depends on the model, and these parameters have changed across releases. I'd confirm the exact field names and supported values against Anthropic's current extended thinking and effort documentation before relying on them."},{"role":"user","content":"friend: debugging is now on and i am testing for thinking compatibility with this prompt"}]} {"headers":{"anthropic-beta":"files-api-2025-04-14"}}
