2b94bd17 [simon@midnightblue.local] why is sky blue?
2b94bd52 [claude-haiku-5-5] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 claude-haiku-5-5 A\ 🪠 🧊 $0.001 495B 3.26s
2b94bd52 [claude-haiku-5-5] #The sky looks blue because of Rayleigh scattering.
2b94bd52 [claude-haiku-5-5] #
2b94bd52 [claude-haiku-5-5] #Sunlight contains all colors of the visible spectrum. As it enters the atmosphere, it collides with
2b94bd52 [claude-haiku-5-5] #gas molecules, mainly nitrogen and oxygen, which are much smaller than the wavelength of light.
2b94bd52 [claude-haiku-5-5] #These molecules scatter shorter wavelengths (blue and violet) much more strongly than longer ones
2b94bd53 [claude-haiku-5-5] #(red and orange). This is because scattering intensity scales roughly with the inverse fourth power
2b94bd53 [claude-haiku-5-5] #of the wavelength.
2b94bd53 [claude-haiku-5-5] #
2b94bd53 [claude-haiku-5-5] #So blue light gets scattered in all directions across the sky, and that scattered light reaches
2b94bd53 [claude-haiku-5-5] #your eyes from every part of the sky, making it look blue. Violet is scattered even more than blue,
2b94bd53 [claude-haiku-5-5] #but the sun emits less violet, and our eyes are less sensitive to it, so the sky appears blue
2b94bd53 [claude-haiku-5-5] #rather than violet.
2b94bd53 [claude-haiku-5-5] #
2b94bd53 [claude-haiku-5-5] #At sunset, sunlight travels through much more atmosphere to reach you. Most of the blue has been
2b94bd53 [claude-haiku-5-5] #scattered away by then, leaving the reds and oranges.
2b94bd53 [claude-haiku-5-5] #
2b94bd53 [roha] anthopic content: [{"type":"text","text":"The sky looks blue because of **Rayleigh scattering**.\n\nSunlight contains all colors of the visible spectrum. As it enters the atmosphere, it collides with gas molecules, mainly nitrogen and oxygen, which are much smaller than the wavelength of light. These molecules scatter shorter wavelengths (blue and violet) much more strongly than longer ones (red and orange). This is because scattering intensity scales roughly with the inverse fourth power of the wavelength.\n\nSo blue light gets scattered in all directions across the sky, and that scattered light reaches your eyes from every part of the sky, making it look blue. Violet is scattered even more than blue, but the sun emits less violet, and our eyes are less sensitive to it, so the sky appears blue rather than violet.\n\nAt sunset, sunlight travels through much more atmosphere to reach you. Most of the blue has been scattered away by then, leaving the reds and oranges."}]
2b94c07c [simon@midnightblue.local] with anthropic api, reply.content has text, if thinking is enabled, will it be part of content block?
2b94c0c2 [claude-haiku-5-5] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 claude-haiku-5-5 A\ 🪠 🧊 $0.002 1.736KB 4.07s
2b94c0c2 [claude-haiku-5-5] #Yes. When extended thinking is enabled, the thinking appears in `content` as separate content
2b94c0c2 [claude-haiku-5-5] #blocks, not as a separate field.
2b94c0c2 [claude-haiku-5-5] #
2b94c0c2 [claude-haiku-5-5] #`content` is a list of blocks, and each block has a `type`:
2b94c0c2 [claude-haiku-5-5] #
2b94c0c2 [claude-haiku-5-5] #- `thinking` blocks contain the model's reasoning in a `thinking` field, along with a
2b94c0c2 [claude-haiku-5-5] #`signature`.
2b94c0c3 [claude-haiku-5-5] #- `redacted_thinking` blocks appear in place of `thinking` blocks when some reasoning is
2b94c0c3 [claude-haiku-5-5] #encrypted for safety reasons.
2b94c0c3 [claude-haiku-5-5] #- `text` blocks contain the visible answer.
2b94c0c3 [claude-haiku-5-5] #
2b94c0c3 [claude-haiku-5-5] #The thinking blocks usually come before the text blocks. So if you read the first block's text
2b94c0c3 [claude-haiku-5-5] #without checking its type, you may get the reasoning instead of the answer. Filter for blocks with
2b94c0c3 [claude-haiku-5-5] #`type == "text"` to get the reply.
2b94c0c3 [claude-haiku-5-5] #
2b94c0c3 [claude-haiku-5-5] #If you're using tool use with extended thinking, pass the thinking blocks back unchanged in the
