2b966f30 [roha] [ASSISTANT] {"role":"assistant","mut":"gemini-4-argon","emoji":"🌟","name":"models/gemini-4-argon","content":"Good day. Yes, there are two main ways to enable reasoning out loud with the Google Generative AI SDK, depending on which Gemini model you are using.\n\n### 1. Native Thinking Mode (Gemini 2.0 and newer)\nGoogle has introduced native \"thinking\" models (such as `gemini-2.0-flash-thinking-exp`) designed specifically for complex reasoning. With these models, the reasoning process is enabled by default. You can also control whether the thought process is returned in the API response using the `thinkingConfig` parameter inside your generation configuration. \n\nWhen enabled, the model returns its reasoning as a separate part of the response payload, marked with a `thought` boolean property, alongside the final answer.\n\n### 2. Chain-of-Thought Prompting (Standard Models)\nIf you are using standard models (like `gemini-1.5-pro` or `gemini-1.5-flash`), there is no API toggle for reasoning. Instead, you can achieve this by using \"Chain-of-Thought\" prompting. \n\nYou can instruct the model via the `systemInstruction` property—or by prepending text to your user prompt—to explicitly state its reasoning step-by-step before providing the final conclusion. Asking the model to enclose its thoughts in specific tags (such as `<thinking>...</thinking>`) makes it easy to separate the reasoning from the final output in your application.\n\nIf you would like to see a code example for configuring either of these approaches in JavaScript, please let me know and I will gladly provide it.","elapsed":5.64109987499984,"price":0.000256}
