2c3dc80a [gemini-4-argon] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.6 gemini-4-argon 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.000 661B 7.99s
2c3dc80a [gemini-4-argon] #Yes, there are a few simple ways to calculate elapsed time in Bash depending on your exact needs:
2c3dc80a [gemini-4-argon] #
2c3dc80a [gemini-4-argon] #• 1. The `time` keyword*
2c3dc80a [gemini-4-argon] #For the quickest check, simply prepend the word `time` before your command. When the command
2c3dc80a [gemini-4-argon] #finishes, Bash will automatically print the real (total elapsed wall-clock time),
2c3dc80a [gemini-4-argon] #user (CPU time spent in user-mode), and sys (CPU time spent in kernel-mode) times
2c3dc80a [gemini-4-argon] #to standard error.
2c3dc80a [gemini-4-argon] #• Pros: Built-in, offers millisecond precision, requires no setup.
2c3dc80a [gemini-4-argon] #• Tip: You can customize its output format by setting the `TIMEFORMAT` environment variable
2c3dc80a [gemini-4-argon] #before running it.
2c3dc80a [gemini-4-argon] #
2c3dc80a [gemini-4-argon] #• 2. The `$SECONDS` variable*
2c3dc80a [gemini-4-argon] #Bash has a built-in internal variable called `SECONDS` that tracks the number of whole seconds
2c3dc80a [gemini-4-argon] #since the shell (or script) was started. If you reset `SECONDS=0` right before your command and
2c3dc80a [gemini-4-argon] #read `$SECONDS` right after, it will contain the elapsed time.
2c3dc80a [gemini-4-argon] #• Pros: Very easy to capture the value into a variable or log file for later use in a
2c3dc80a [gemini-4-argon] #script.
2c3dc80a [gemini-4-argon] #• Cons: Only measures in whole seconds (no millisecond precision).
2c3dc80a [gemini-4-argon] #
2c3dc80a [gemini-4-argon] #• 3. `EPOCHREALTIME` (Bash 5.0+)*
2c3dc80a [gemini-4-argon] #If you need to store the elapsed time in a variable and require sub-second precision, Bash
2c3dc80a [gemini-4-argon] #5 introduced `EPOCHREALTIME`, which gives you the current Unix epoch time in microseconds. You can
2c3dc80a [gemini-4-argon] #capture this value before and after your command, and then pass the values to a tool like `bc` or
2c3dc80a [gemini-4-argon] #`awk` to subtract them.
2c3dc80a [gemini-4-argon] #
2c3dc80a [gemini-4-argon] #• 4. The `date` command*
2c3dc80a [gemini-4-argon] #In older shells or strict POSIX `sh` environments where Bash-specific variables aren't available,
2c3dc80a [gemini-4-argon] #the traditional method is calling the external `date` utility before and after the command (using
2c3dc80a [gemini-4-argon] #`+%s` for seconds or `+%s.%N` on GNU systems for nanoseconds) and subtracting the start time from
2c3dc80a [gemini-4-argon] #the end time.
2c3dc80a [gemini-4-argon] #
2c3dc80a [gemini-4-argon] #If you would like to see code examples or a reusable wrapper function for any of these approaches,
2c3dc80a [gemini-4-argon] #please let me know.
2c3dc80a [gemini-4-argon] #
2c3dd59b [simon@midnightblue.local] time works well, is there an option to just emit real and not user and sys?
2c3dd5f6 [gemini-4-argon] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.6 gemini-4-argon 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.001 2.803KB 5.50s
2c3dd5f6 [gemini-4-argon] #Yes, you can do this by setting the `TIMEFORMAT` environment variable in Bash.
2c3dd5f6 [gemini-4-argon] #
2c3dd5f6 [gemini-4-argon] #By default, `time` prints real, user, and sys, but if you set `TIMEFORMAT` to `%R` (or `%3R` for
2c3dd5f6 [gemini-4-argon] #three decimal places, which is the default precision), it will only output the real elapsed time in
2c3dd5f6 [gemini-4-argon] #seconds.
2c3dd5f6 [gemini-4-argon] #
2c3dd5f6 [gemini-4-argon] #Here are the main format specifiers you can assign to `TIMEFORMAT` for real time:
2c3dd5f6 [gemini-4-argon] #
2c3dd5f6 [gemini-4-argon] #• `%R`: Prints the elapsed time in seconds and milliseconds (e.g., `1.234`).
2c3dd5f6 [gemini-4-argon] #• `%lR`: Prints the elapsed time in a longer `minutes` and `seconds` format (e.g.,
2c3dd5f6 [gemini-4-argon] #`0m1.234s`), which matches the standard `time` output style.
2c3dd5f6 [gemini-4-argon] #• `%0R`: Prints only whole seconds, omitting the decimal places.
2c3dd5f6 [gemini-4-argon] #
2c3dd5f6 [gemini-4-argon] #You can also include custom text in the variable (for example, setting `TIMEFORMAT="Elapsed: %R
2c3dd5f6 [gemini-4-argon] #seconds"`). Once set in your script or terminal session, every subsequent call to `time` will
2c3dd5f6 [gemini-4-argon] #strictly use that format.
2c3dd5f6 [gemini-4-argon] #
2c3dd5f6 [gemini-4-argon] #If you would like to see a syntax example of how to set this globally or scope it to a single
2c3dd5f6 [gemini-4-argon] #command, just let me know.
2c3dd5f7 [gemini-4-argon] #
